            <span style="position: absolute; top: 0.75rem; left: 0.75rem; background: #003853; color: #fff; font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; padding: 0.3rem 0.75rem; border-radius: 50px;">Coastal Swahili</span>
          </div>
          <div style="padding: 1.5rem;">
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #003853;">Swahili (Zanzibari)</h3>
            <p class="tribe-region">Zanzibar, Pemba & Coastal Tanzania</p>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #4e5d66; line-height: 1.5;">The Swahili people are the product of 1,000+ years of Indian Ocean trade — a fusion of Bantu African roots with Arab, Persian, and Indian merchants. Their culture is defined by Islam, coral-stone architecture, intricate carved doors, taarab music, and a cuisine rich in spices, coconut, and seafood.</p>
            <ul class="tribe-highlights">
              <li>Population: ~500K on Zanzibar</li>
              <li>Language: Kiswahili (native)</li>
              <li>Key experience: Stone Town walking tour, spice farm</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="tribe-card reveal delay-3">
          <div class="tribe-icon">🌾</div>
          <h3>Chagga</h3>
          <p class="tribe-region">Kilimanjaro Region (Moshi, Marangu)</p>
          <p>Traditional farmers of the Kilimanjaro slopes, the Chagga are known for their sophisticated irrigation systems, banana-coffee farming, and the historic Chagga caves used as refuge during tribal wars. They were among the first Tanzanians to adopt Christianity and formal education.</p>
          <ul class="tribe-highlights">
            <li>Population: ~2 million</li>
            <li>Language: Kichagga</li>
            <li>Key experience: Coffee tour, Chagga caves visit</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="tribe-card reveal delay-4">
          <div class="tribe-icon">🎨</div>
          <h3>Makonde</h3>
          <p class="tribe-region">Southeast Tanzania (Mtwara, Lindi) & Northern Mozambique</p>
          <p>Master woodcarvers famous for their intricate ebony and African blackwood sculptures. The Makonde are also known for their elaborate initiation ceremonies (unyago) involving face masks, dance, and body scarification. Their art is displayed in museums worldwide.</p>
          <ul class="tribe-highlights">
            <li>Population: ~1.5 million</li>
            <li>Language: Chimakonde</li>
            <li>Key experience: Carving workshop visit, Mtwara market</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="tribe-card reveal delay-5">
          <div class="tribe-icon">🏔️</div>
          <h3>Hadza (Hadzabe)</h3>
          <p class="tribe-region">Lake Eyasi Region (near Ngorongoro)</p>
          <p>One of Africa's last remaining hunter-gatherer societies. The Hadza have lived in the Lake Eyasi basin for millennia, speaking a unique click language unrelated to any other. Fewer than 1,300 remain, with only ~300 still practicing traditional foraging.</p>
          <ul class="tribe-highlights">
            <li>Population: ~1,300 total</li>
            <li>Language: Hadzane (click language)</li>
            <li>Key experience: Hunting walk, fire-making demo</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="tribe-card reveal delay-6">
          <div class="tribe-icon">🐄</div>
          <h3>Datoga</h3>
          <p class="tribe-region">Lake Eyasi & Manyara Regions</p>
          <p>Pastoralists and skilled blacksmiths who neighbor the Hadza. The Datoga forge tools, jewelry, and arrowheads using traditional bellows and open fires. Their facial scarification patterns and brass jewelry are distinctive cultural markers.</p>
          <ul class="tribe-highlights">
            <li>Population: ~87,000</li>
            <li>Language: Datoga</li>
            <li>Key experience: Blacksmith workshop, beadwork demo</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
