      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <span class="section-tag">Zanzibar Culture</span>
        <h2>Swahili Culture Deep Dive</h2>
        <p>Zanzibar's unique identity shaped by centuries of Indian Ocean trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="culture-grid">
        <div class="culture-card reveal">
          <h3>Language & Greetings</h3>
          <p>Kiswahili is the national language. Learning a few phrases goes a long way:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Jambo / Mambo</strong> — Hello (informal)</li>
            <li><strong>Habari</strong> — How are you? / News?</li>
            <li><strong>Asante</strong> — Thank you</li>
            <li><strong>Karibu</strong> — Welcome</li>
            <li><strong>Samahani</strong> — Excuse me / Sorry</li>
            <li><strong>Hakuna matata</strong> — No worries (yes, it's real!)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="culture-card reveal delay-2">
          <h3>Religion & Ramadan</h3>
          <p>~99% of Zanzibaris are Muslim (predominantly Sunni). During Ramadan, daily life shifts — restaurants may close during daylight, and eating/drinking in public is respectful to avoid. The call to prayer (adhan) echoes five times daily from mosques across the islands.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="culture-card reveal delay-3">
          <h3>Architecture & Doors</h3>
          <p>Stone Town's UNESCO-listed architecture features coral-stone buildings with distinctive carved wooden doors. Door styles indicate the owner's heritage: Indian doors have brass spikes (against war elephants); Arab doors feature Quranic inscriptions and geometric patterns; Swahili doors blend both traditions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="culture-card reveal delay-4">
          <h3>Music: Taarab & Kidumbak</h3>
          <p>Taarab is Zanzibar's classical orchestral music — a fusion of Arab oud, Indian tabla, and African rhythms, sung in Swahili poetry. Kidumbak is its faster, more percussive cousin. The Dhow Countries Music Academy in Stone Town offers performances and lessons.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="culture-card reveal delay-5">
          <h3>Cuisine: The Spice Island Kitchen</h3>
          <p>Zanzibari food tells the trade story: biryani and pilau from India; coconut-based curries from the coast; grilled seafood with pili pili (chili) sauce; urojo (Zanzibar street soup) at Forodhani Gardens; and spices — cloves, cinnamon, cardamom, nutmeg — in everything.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="culture-card reveal delay-6">
          <h3>Festivals & Celebrations</h3>
          <p><strong>Sauti za Busara</strong> (February) — East Africa's premier music festival in Stone Town's Old Fort. <strong>Zanzibar International Film Festival</strong> (July) — African cinema showcase. <strong>Eid al-Fitr & Eid al-Adha</strong> — Major Islamic holidays with feasting, new clothes, and family visits. <strong>Mwaka Kogwa</strong> (July) — Shirazi New Year in Makunduchi with mock fights and banana-stem burning.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

