  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header reveal">
        <span class="section-tag">Responsible Tourism</span>
        <h2>How to Experience Culture Respectfully</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="tips-list reveal">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Choose community-run programs</strong> — Ask who owns the experience and where the money goes. We only partner with cooperatives and community-owned operations.</li>
          <li><strong>Dress modestly</strong> — In villages and Stone Town, cover shoulders and knees. Beachwear is for beaches only.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask before photographing</strong> — Always seek permission before taking photos of people, especially in rural areas. Some Maasai believe photos steal the soul.</li>
          <li><strong>Support local artisans directly</strong> — Buy crafts from makers at village cooperatives, not tourist shops. Prices are fairer and money stays in the community.</li>
          <li><strong>Learn basic greetings</strong> — A simple "Jambo" or "Habari" opens doors and shows respect.</li>
          <li><strong>Be mindful during Ramadan</strong> — Don't eat, drink, or smoke in public during daylight hours. Many local restaurants close until sunset.</li>
          <li><strong>Don't give candy or money to children</strong> — It encourages begging and school absenteeism. Support schools and community projects through established channels instead.</li>
          <li><strong>Respect sacred sites</strong> — Mosques, shrines, and ancestral sites may have restrictions. Follow your guide's instructions.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

