        <h2>Cultural Questions Answered</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-grid">
        <details class="faq-item reveal">
          <summary class="faq-question">Is it ethical to visit Maasai villages?</summary>
          <p class="faq-answer">It can be, <strong>if done right</strong>. Choose community-owned programs where the village council controls the tourism income and decides how many visitors they accept. Avoid "drive-by" photo stops organized by outside operators. We only work with Maasai cooperatives that meet these standards.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item reveal delay-2">
          <summary class="faq-question">Can I visit the Hadza?</summary>
          <p class="faq-answer">Yes, but only through <strong>strictly vetted operators</strong>. The Hadza are extremely vulnerable — fewer than 300 still hunt and gather traditionally. We partner with organizations that have long-term relationships, pay fair community fees, limit group sizes, and ensure visits don't disrupt daily life. Never visit independently.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item reveal delay-3">
          <summary class="faq-question">What should I wear in Zanzibar towns?</summary>
          <p class="faq-answer">Modest clothing: shoulders and knees covered for both men and women. Loose, light fabrics are ideal for the heat. Beachwear (bikinis, board shorts, tank tops) is only appropriate on beaches and at resort pools. In Stone Town, you'll be more comfortable and respected in a light shirt and trousers or a long skirt/dress.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item reveal delay-4">
          <summary class="faq-question">Do I need to speak Swahili?</summary>
          <p class="faq-answer">English is widely spoken in tourist areas, hotels, and by guides. However, learning 5–10 basic Swahili phrases transforms interactions — vendors smile, children wave, and doors open. It shows respect for the culture. We provide a phrase card with all our cultural tours.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item reveal delay-5">
          <summary class="faq-question">What's the deal with "Hakuna Matata"?</summary>
          <p class="faq-answer">It's real Swahili! <em>Hakuna</em> = "there is not" and <em>matata</em> = "problems/worries." It's used casually like "no worries" or "it's all good." The Lion King made it famous globally, but locals use it daily. You'll hear it constantly — especially when something goes wrong and everyone stays calm.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item reveal delay-6">
          <summary class="faq-question">Can I attend a traditional ceremony?</summary>
          <p class="faq-answer">Some ceremonies are open to respectful visitors (weddings, Eid celebrations, Mwaka Kogwa). Others are strictly private (initiation rites, certain spiritual ceremonies). Your guide will advise what's appropriate. Never assume you're welcome — always ask.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

